now the nobel peace prize for this year  has been awarded to two journalists  maria ressa of the philippines and  dmitry muratov in russia  ms ressa and mr muratov  are receiving the peace prize for their  courageous fight  for freedom of expression in the  philippines  and in russia  at the same time  they are representatives  of all journalists  who stand up for this idea in a world in  which democracy and freedom of the press  face  increasingly adverse conditions  well let's bring in our asia  correspondent siobhan robbins who is in  bangkok and diana magnay who is in  moscow for siobhan to you first maria  ressa a journalist in the philippines  i've met her she's fearless isn't she  scrutinizing at the government of  rodrigo duterte just tell us a bit more  about her  well sam you know all too well how  fearless she is she's a real firecracker  she's a veteran journalist veteran  correspondent and in 2012 she co-founded  the online  news site rappler  but really the pressure that she's been  facing in the philippines over the last  few years intensified following the  election of rodrigo duterte and her with  the rappler staff did intensive  investigations into  extrajudicial killings human rights  abuses linked to duterte's war on drugs  also exposing what she said was an  online  troll army basically which was pushing  fake news around his presidency and  since then both her and the rappler  staff have faced more than a dozen legal  cases yet it hasn't deterred her she's  continued to fight for freedom of the  press and it's interesting that today  when she was actually getting that award  she was online doing an online forum a  chat about freedom of the press and you  see her suddenly saying oh someone's  calling me a few minutes she comes back  and she just says that she's speechless  uh that it just shows how tough it is  and that this is for all of the other  journalists in the philippines and i  think many journalists in the  philippines will be buoyed by this  because many are reporting that it's  becoming extremely hard for them to do  their job and this was her message to  them  journalism has never been as important  as it is today and yet it is so  difficult to do the job that we had set  out um  in order to keep doing what we're doing  keep the north star ahead of you you  know you  protect the facts  you hold power to account  you  exercise the rights that is in the  philippine constitution  that's what we did and that's what we'll  keep doing  she's keeping on going isn't she diana  let's talk about the other journalist  recipient the editor of an independent  russian newspaper which has seen several  of its journalists killed  that's right he's editor-in-chief of  novi gazietta which is really the uh  most well-known longest established  independent newspaper pretty much the  only independent uh outlet that has  still not been labeled a foreign agent  in what has over the last few months  been a real  escalating crackdown on  the freedom of the press here in russia  and as you said navier gazietta it was  established in 1993 and dmitry muratif  was one of its  founders back in the day when they  really didn't have much beyond a couple  of computers and a few  seats and interestingly and quite a nice  symmetry um michael gorbachev who owns  part of novio gazietta donated part of  his nobel peace prize prize money to  novio gazetta as it was getting going um  to to to to get it going in that time  six of novio gazetta's journalists have  been killed doing uh their work which  has been a really fearless  um endeavor to expose corruption to  expose human rights abuses  yesterday marked the 15th anniversary of  probably the most famous of those  reporters anna polikovskaya's murder  here in moscow she had been probing for  a long time human rights abuses in  chechnya and that 15-year mark  marks the expiration of the statute of  limitations on  her murder no one has ever been held  accountable and we heard today from  dmitry muratov that this prize for him  is is not just for him it is for all of  those who've def who've died uh  defending their  all of our right to freedom of  expression and he also said that he was  going to give some of that prime prize  money to  children with uh spinal muscular  dystrophy  um and also to journalistic activities  and that he would do what he could to  fight for all those other outlets who've  been called foreign agents because just  as in the philippines it really is a  very very tough time for independent  journalism in russia the authorities are  very much cracking down on it every  friday practically we've seen more media  outlets named uh foreign agents will be  interesting to see if navigator manages  to escape that label um today the  kremlin uh commented on uh his award and  called him a talented courageous  journalist i don't think that is a label  that um mr muratov is particularly going  to  care about what the kremlin says because  as the committee said he has stood  fearlessly uh and championed in  independence uh and a continuation of  holding the government to account  and exposing abuses of the regime  throughout his more than 20-year career  at a very very difficult time in russia  and i struggle to think of any other  outlet journalistic outlet in the world  which has seen six of its reporters  killed and i think that shows you um  just  the kind of work that navia gazetta does  here  okay diana in moscow siobhan in bangkok  thank you very much the nobel peace  prize this year just to reiterate  awarded to two journalists maria ressa  of the philippines and dimitri muratov  in russia
 

